Cargo goes down with its ship: a ship that is lost (sunk in battle, disbanded, or disbanded by patch 0007's broke AI) took nothing with it. GameData.RemoveUnit left the units aboard on the water, still loaded on a ship that no longer existed, with no route anywhere: a land unit stranded at sea for the rest of the game. Now, as in Civilization III, a ship lost at sea loses its passengers with it, and a ship lost in a city puts its passengers ashore there, awake. Since removing a ship can now remove more than one unit, the loops that remove units one by one allow for it: a destroyed civilization's units are removed from a copy of the list (walking the list itself by index ran past its end and threw when a passenger was listed before its ship, leaving the captured city in place), and a broke human seat stops disbanding when it has no units left. The broke AI spares a loaded ship at sea as it spares settlers and workers, so it does not drown the settler it is ferrying.

